User Story – Publish Returned Equipment Data to the SharePoint Return List

Title

Publish returned equipment data to the SharePoint Return list with one JSON payload per visit

User Story

As a Product Owner / reporting owner for Le Comptoir,
I want the application to publish returned equipment information into a dedicated SharePoint Return list,
so that Power Automate can parse the JSON payload, create one Dataverse row per returned equipment item, and make the data reliably available for Power BI reporting.

⸻

Context

Today, returned equipment information is stored directly in the visit data in a compact format, mainly through a list of RITMs and equipment types.

This approach is becoming limiting because we now want to retrieve much more information for each returned equipment item, for example:

* RITM
* Asset Tag
* Equipment type
* Model
* Serial number
* Owner
* Depositor
* HAM information
* Return request information
* etc.

To avoid making the Visit object too heavy and to simplify downstream processing, a dedicated SharePoint Return list will be used as a staging layer.

⸻

Expected Functional Behaviour

General Principle

For each visit containing at least one returned equipment item:

1 visit
=
1 row in the SharePoint Return list

The SharePoint row must contain:

VisitId
+
JSON payload containing all returned equipment items for the visit

Example:

Visit ABC123
├── Workstation
├── Display Device
└── Accessory

must create:

1 SharePoint Return item

with a JSON payload containing the 3 returned equipment items.

⸻

SharePoint Publication

The application must publish the data to:

SharePoint List: Return

The publication must be performed through:

Microsoft Graph API

using:

JSON batching

to reduce network round trips and avoid unnecessary API calls.

Batching Rule

Visits to be published should be grouped into Graph batches.

Maximum: 20 sub-requests per Graph batch

Each sub-request represents:

1 visit containing returned equipment
=
1 SharePoint Return item creation/update

Example:

Graph Batch
Request 1  → Visit ABC001
Request 2  → Visit ABC002
Request 3  → Visit ABC003
...
Request 20 → Visit ABC020

A visit containing several equipment items must still generate only one SharePoint item / one Graph sub-request.

⸻

Expected SharePoint Structure

The Return list must at least contain:

Field	Description
VisitId	Unique Le Comptoir visit identifier
PayloadJson	JSON payload containing all returned equipment items for the visit

Additional technical fields may be added for downstream processing, for example:

ProcessingStatus
ProcessedAt
RetryCount
ErrorMessage
SourceVersion

These technical fields do not necessarily need to be populated by the application.

⸻

JSON Payload

The JSON payload must contain the collection of returned equipment items linked to the visit.

General expected structure:

{
  "visitId": "<VisitId>",
  "items": [
    {
      "...": "..."
    },
    {
      "...": "..."
    }
  ]
}

Expected Properties

To be completed with the JSON export of the Return Equipment collection provided by the application.

Paste the reference JSON below:

{
  "items": [
    {
      "FIELDS_TO_BE_COMPLETED": "..."
    }
  ]
}

The JSON attached to this User Story will be the reference for the properties that must be published.

⸻

Downstream Processing

The application is not responsible for normalising the returned equipment into separate Dataverse rows.

The application is only responsible for:

Collecting
↓
Structuring
↓
Publishing the JSON payload

The transformation will be handled by Power Automate:

SharePoint Return
        ↓
Power Automate
        ↓
Parse JSON
        ↓
Apply to each items[]
        ↓
Dataverse ReturnedEquipment

In Dataverse:

1 returned equipment item
=
1 row

Example:

SharePoint
Visit ABC123
Payload containing 3 equipment items

becomes:

Dataverse ReturnedEquipment
Row 1 → Equipment item 1
Row 2 → Equipment item 2
Row 3 → Equipment item 3

⸻

Acceptance Criteria

AC1 – One SharePoint row per visit

Given a visit contains one or more returned equipment items
When the application publishes the return data
Then only one row must be created in the SharePoint Return list for that visit.

⸻

AC2 – No Return item when no equipment is returned

Given a visit contains no returned equipment item
When the visit is processed
Then no row must be created in the SharePoint Return list.

⸻

AC3 – All equipment items included in the JSON

Given a visit contains multiple returned equipment items
When the JSON payload is generated
Then every item from the Return Equipment collection must be included in the items array.

⸻

AC4 – VisitId must be included

Each SharePoint Return item must contain:

VisitId

so that all returned equipment items can later be linked back to the original visit.

⸻

AC5 – JSON structure must match the agreed reference

All properties defined in the reference JSON added to this User Story must be published.

Property names must be:

Stable
Consistent
Independent from UI labels

⸻

AC6 – Graph API batching

SharePoint publications must support Microsoft Graph JSON batching.

A batch must not exceed:

20 sub-requests

⸻

AC7 – Each Graph sub-response must be checked individually

The application must validate the result of every individual sub-request.

A successful overall batch response must not be interpreted as meaning every sub-request succeeded.

⸻

AC8 – Throttling handling

In case of:

429

or:

503

the application must respect:

Retry-After

and retry only the failed publication(s).

⸻

AC9 – No data loss

A visit must not be considered successfully published until its SharePoint Return item has been successfully created or updated.

⸻

AC10 – No duplicate Return rows

A retry must not generate multiple SharePoint Return rows for the same visit.

The logical idempotency key is:

VisitId

⸻

Out of Scope

This User Story does not cover:

Power Automate JSON parsing
Creation of Dataverse ReturnedEquipment rows
Power BI reporting
Historical data migration

These items can be handled through dedicated User Stories / technical tasks.

⸻

Expected Result

Application
   ↓
Return Equipment collection
   ↓
Generate one JSON payload per visit
   ↓
Graph API batching
   ↓
SharePoint Return
   ↓
1 row = 1 visit
   ↓
JSON payload = N returned equipment items

Then downstream:

Power Automate
   ↓
Parse JSON
   ↓
Dataverse ReturnedEquipment
   ↓
1 row = 1 returned equipment item

Functional Summary

When a visit contains returned equipment, the application must publish a single row into the SharePoint Return list. This row contains the VisitId and a JSON payload describing all returned equipment items for that visit. Publications must support Microsoft Graph batching with up to 20 sub-requests per batch. The application must not split equipment items into separate SharePoint rows; this responsibility is delegated to Power Automate, which will parse the JSON and populate the ReturnedEquipment Dataverse table with one row per returned equipment item.
